mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Private Two-User Messenger — Backend API\n\nThis canister is the backend for a private, two-person messaging application. It stores a single one-to-one conversation between exactly two pre-authorized accounts: text messages with delivery and read status, reactions, attachments, presence (online/offline), typing indicators, and a one-to-one call history with WebRTC signaling (SDP offers/answers and ICE candidates). There is no public registration, no group chat, and no third-party participant.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\nThe app authenticates with Internet Identity through the `MixinAuthorization` mixin. The first non-anonymous caller to register becomes the `admin`; every later caller that registers becomes a `user`. Anonymous callers are always treated as `guest` and are never registered.\n\nRegistration happens only when a caller signs in through the app's own frontend (which calls `_internet_identity_sign_in_start` / `_internet_identity_sign_in_finish`). A direct API caller can register by calling `_initialize_access_control` once as a signed-in (non-anonymous) caller before any role-guarded call. The first caller to register receives the `admin` role; all subsequent callers receive `user`. A caller can therefore be unregistered even when the app already knows it: registration only occurs through the frontend sign-in flow, so a principal that never signed in is unregistered even if it belongs to the app's owner, and a signed-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal than the one the frontend registered.\n\n- `_internet_identity_sign_in_start() : async Blob` — issues a sign-in challenge. Anonymous callers may call it.\n- `_internet_identity_sign_in_finish() : async Result<(), Verify.Error>` — verifies the challenge and registers the caller (first caller becomes `admin`, later callers become `user`).\n- `_initialize_access_control() : async ()` — registers the caller without attribute verification. Anonymous callers are ignored (they remain unregistered).\n- `getCallerUserRole() : async UserRole` — returns `#guest` for an anonymous caller; returns the caller's role for a registered caller; traps with `\"User is not registered\"` for a non-anonymous caller that has not registered.\n- `assignCallerUserRole(user : Principal, role : UserRole) : async ()` — admin-only. Traps with `\"Unauthorized: Only admins can assign user roles\"` when the caller is not an admin.\n- `isCallerAdmin() : async Bool` — returns whether the caller is an admin.\n\nThe app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin, for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`. Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n\nEvery public messaging and calls method below authorizes the caller twice: it must have the `user` role (or higher) AND be one of the two principals in the two-account allow-list (`authorizedUsers`). A caller that fails either check traps with `\"Unauthorized: Only authorized users ...\"` or `\"Unauthorized: Only the two pre-authorized accounts ...\"`. Anonymous and unregistered callers are rejected, and a registered third party who is not in the allow-list is also rejected, so only the two pre-authorized signed-in accounts can access the conversation, its media, and its call signaling.\n\nThe allow-list is configured by the admin:\n\n- `setAuthorizedUsers(users : [UserId]) : async ()` — admin-only. Sets the two pre-authorized principals. Traps with `\"Unauthorized: Only the admin can set the authorized users\"` when the caller is not an admin, and with `\"Authorized users must be exactly two principals\"` when the array does not contain exactly two principals. Until this is called, `authorizedUsers` is empty and no caller passes the allow-list check.\n- `getAuthorizedUsers() : async [UserId]` — returns the current two-account allow-list. Requires the caller to be an authorized user.\n- `getPartner() : async ?UserId` — returns the other authorized principal (the caller's conversation partner), or `null` if the allow-list is not yet set to exactly two principals or the caller is not one of them. This lets the frontend determine the partner's principal to initiate calls and show presence even before the partner has sent a message.\n\n## Data access (OQL)\n\nThe persisted queryable collections are exposed through the OQL `schema()` and `execute()` methods for the Data Intelligence agent. Every entity is `controllerOnly`: only the platform controller (the agent) can read them, so the private conversation data is never readable by end users through the query layer.\n\n- `schema() : async Text` — returns the JSON schema of the exposed entities.\n- `execute(json : Text) : async Text` — runs a JSON query against the exposed entities.\n\nExposed entities:\n- `message` (primary key `id`) — fields `id`, `sender`, `text`, `timestamp`, `status`, `replyTo`.\n- `call` (primary key `id`) — fields `id`, `caller`, `callee`, `startedAt`, `endedAt`, `status`.\n- `presence` (primary key `userId`) — fields `userId`, `online`, `updatedAt`.\n- `typing` (primary key `userId`) — fields `userId`, `isTyping`, `updatedAt`.\n\n## Messaging\n\n- `setAuthorizedUsers(users : [UserId]) : async ()` — admin-only; sets the two pre-authorized principals (see Authentication and authorization).\n- `getAuthorizedUsers() : async [UserId]` — returns the two-account allow-list.\n- `getPartner() : async ?UserId` — returns the caller's conversation partner, or `null` if not determinable.\n- `sendMessage(text : Text, replyTo : ?MessageId, attachment : ?Attachment) : async MessageId` — creates a message and returns its id.\n- `listMessages() : async [Message]` — returns all messages in the conversation. This is an update call: it also marks every message sent by the other user and still in `#sent` status as `#delivered`, so fetching the conversation advances the delivery state.\n- `getMessage(id : MessageId) : async ?Message` — returns one message, or `null` if absent.\n- `deleteMessage(id : MessageId) : async Bool` — deletes a message; returns whether it was removed.\n- `reactToMessage(id : MessageId, emoji : Text) : async Bool` — adds a reaction to a message; returns whether it succeeded.\n- `searchMessages(term : Text) : async [Message]` — returns messages whose text matches `term`.\n- `markRead() : async ()` — marks the conversation as read by the caller (messages from the other user become `#read`).\n- `setPresence(online : Bool) : async ()` — records the caller's online/offline presence.\n- `getPresence(userId : UserId) : async ?Presence` — returns the other user's presence record, or `null` if none is recorded.\n- `setTyping(isTyping : Bool) : async ()` — records whether the caller is currently typing.\n- `getTyping(userId : UserId) : async ?TypingStatus` — returns the other user's typing status, or `null` if none is recorded.\n\n## Calls and WebRTC signaling\n\n- `startCall(callee : UserId) : async CallId` — starts a call to the given callee and returns its id. The new call is created in `#ringing` status.\n- `acceptCall(id : CallId) : async Bool` — accepts a ringing call, transitioning it from `#ringing` to `#ongoing`; returns whether the call was found and was still ringing. Accepting a call that is not `#ringing` (for example one already `#ongoing` or `#ended`) is a no-op that returns `false`.\n- `endCall(id : CallId) : async Bool` — ends a call and returns whether it was found and transitioned. An `#ongoing` call becomes `#ended`; a `#ringing` call becomes `#missed` when the caller ends it (the callee never answered) or `#declined` when the callee ends it. Ending a call already in a terminal state (`#ended`, `#missed`, `#declined`) is a no-op that returns `false`.\n- `listCalls() : async [Call]` — returns the call history.\n- `getCall(id : CallId) : async ?Call` — returns one call, or `null` if absent.\n- `setOffer(callId : CallId, sdp : Text) : async ()` — stores the caller's SDP offer for the call (overwrites any previous offer).\n- `getOffer(callId : CallId) : async ?Text` — returns the stored SDP offer for the call, or `null` if none.\n- `setAnswer(callId : CallId, sdp : Text) : async ()` — stores the caller's SDP answer for the call (overwrites any previous answer).\n- `getAnswer(callId : CallId) : async ?Text` — returns the stored SDP answer for the call, or `null` if none.\n- `addIceCandidate(callId : CallId, candidate : Text) : async ()` — appends an ICE candidate to the call's candidate list.\n- `getIceCandidates(callId : CallId) : async [Text]` — returns all ICE candidates stored for the call (empty if none).\n\nThe signaling methods let two devices exchange WebRTC session descriptions and ICE candidates through the backend: the caller stores an offer with `setOffer`, the callee reads it with `getOffer`, stores an answer with `setAnswer`, and both sides exchange ICE candidates with `addIceCandidate` / `getIceCandidates`. Each method authorizes the caller, so only the two accounts can read or write signaling for a call.\n\n## Units and encodings\n\n- `UserId` is a `Principal` (the caller's Internet Identity principal).\n- `MessageId` and `CallId` are `Nat` counters, allocated monotonically by the canister.\n- `Timestamp` is an `Int` count of nanoseconds since the Unix epoch (the value of `Time.now()`).\n- `MessageStatus` is a variant: `#sent`, `#delivered`, `#read`.\n- `CallStatus` is a variant: `#ringing`, `#ongoing`, `#ended`, `#missed`, `#declined`.\n- `replyTo` is an optional `MessageId`; `null` means the message is not a reply.\n- `endedAt` is an optional `Timestamp`; `null` means the call has not ended.\n- `Attachment` is an optional record `{ id : Nat; kind : Text; url : Text; name : Text; size : Nat }`; `null` means the message has no attachment.\n- `Reaction` is a record `{ emoji : Text; author : UserId }`.\n- SDP offers, SDP answers, and ICE candidates are opaque `Text` values (WebRTC session descriptions and candidate strings) that the backend stores and returns verbatim; it does not parse or interpret them.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\nMessages, calls, and signaling are created by update calls and are immediately durable. Read-only methods (`getMessage`, `searchMessages`, `getPresence`, `getTyping`, `getCall`, `getOffer`, `getAnswer`, `getIceCandidates`, `listCalls`, `getCallerUserRole`, `isCallerAdmin`, `schema`) are queries and can be polled freely. Mutating methods (`sendMessage`, `deleteMessage`, `reactToMessage`, `markRead`, `setPresence`, `setTyping`, `startCall`, `acceptCall`, `endCall`, `setOffer`, `setAnswer`, `addIceCandidate`) are updates and should be called once per user action; poll the corresponding read method to observe the result rather than re-issuing the mutation. `listMessages` is an update (it advances delivery state) and should be called once per fetch.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n`sendMessage` and `startCall` allocate a fresh id on each call, so a retried call creates a new message or call rather than overwriting an existing one. `deleteMessage`, `reactToMessage`, `acceptCall`, and `endCall` are idempotent in effect: they act on a specific id and return whether the target was found and transitioned, so re-calling them after success is a no-op that returns `false`. `setPresence`, `setTyping`, `setOffer`, `setAnswer`, and `addIceCandidate` are idempotent state writes; `setOffer`/`setAnswer` overwrite the stored value, and `addIceCandidate` appends to the call's candidate list (re-sending the same candidate duplicates it). `markRead` and `listMessages` are idempotent: re-running them leaves already-advanced statuses unchanged.\n\n## Errors, traps, and limits\n\n- `getCallerUserRole` traps with `\"User is not registered\"` for a non-anonymous, unregistered caller.\n- `assignCallerUserRole` traps with `\"Unauthorized: Only admins can assign user roles\"` when the caller is not an admin.\n- `setAuthorizedUsers` traps with `\"Unauthorized: Only the admin can set the authorized users\"` when the caller is not an admin, and with `\"Authorized users must be exactly two principals\"` when the array length is not two.\n- Every messaging and calls method traps with `\"Unauthorized: Only authorized users ...\"` when the caller is not a registered `user` (or admin), and with `\"Unauthorized: Only the two pre-authorized accounts ...\"` when the caller is not in the two-account allow-list. A registered third party who is not in the allow-list is therefore rejected.\n- `getPartner` returns `null` when the allow-list is not set to exactly two principals or the caller is not one of them.\n- `getMessage`, `getCall`, `getOffer`, `getAnswer`, `getPresence`, and `getTyping` return `null` (not an error) when the target does not exist.\n- `deleteMessage`, `reactToMessage`, `acceptCall`, and `endCall` return `false` when the target id does not exist or the call is not in a state that can be transitioned.\n- `getIceCandidates` returns an empty array when no candidates are stored.\n- The conversation is private: only the two pre-authorized accounts participate, and the OQL entities are controller-only so the data is not exposed to arbitrary users.\n"
  };
};
